.TH "shtoolkit" "7" "July 2026" "BetterScripts" "BetterScripts \f[V]shtoolkit\f[R] common docs."
.hy
.SS \f[V]shtoolkit\f[R]
.RS
.PP
Previously known as \f[I]BetterScripts POSIX Suite\f[R].
.RE
.PP
An ever growing suite of libraries which aim to make portable scripting
easier, safer, and more efficient written for scripts written for
\[lq]command interpretation services and common utility programs\[rq] as
defined in the \[lq]Shell and
Utilities\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/idx/xcu.html)
volume of the
\f[I]POSIX.1-2008\f[R] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition)
standard.
.SS DESCRIPTION
.PP
The suite libraries provide commands that enable commonly undertaken
tasks to be accomplished more easily, while maintaining maximum
portability, by providing more advanced features on top of the
\f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] standard, and providing workarounds for environments
or utilities which can cause problems.
.PP
To the extent possible, every library in the suite is self-contained and
has no dependencies outside the library except for a compatible
\f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] environment.
.PP
While some libraries may provide for being directly executed (i.e.\ as
indistinguishable from a binary command), most libraries are intended to
be
\[lq]sourced\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/utilities/V3_chap02.html#dot)
into any script where the library commands will then be available.
.SS LIBRARIES
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.PP
\f[V]libarray.sh\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Emulated arrays for any shell.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.PP
\f[V]libdeque.sh\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Emulated double ended queues, queues, and stacks for any shell.
.IP \[bu] 2
The use of these is recommended ahead of other data types
(e.g.\ emulated arrays) wherever possible as they are much faster.
.IP \[bu] 2
Both queues and stacks are specializations of double ended queues and so
are provided in the same library.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.PP
\f[V]libgetargs.sh\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Argument processing made easy - like \f[V]getopt\f[R] or
\f[V]getopts\f[R] but much more powerful, while requiring less work.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.PP
\f[V]getarg\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Wrapper for \f[V]libgetargs.sh\f[R] which allows invoking without
needing to be sourced.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.PP
\f[V]libmap.sh\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Emulated associative arrays for any shell.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.PP
\f[V]libpath.sh\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Commands to assist with path processing.
.IP \[bu] 2
Includes commands to retrieve path information like size, owner, etc.
.IP \[bu] 2
Provides a portable \f[V]mktemp\f[R] alternative.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.PP
\f[V]mktmp\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Wrapper for \f[V]libpath.sh\f[R] which allows invoking the
\f[V]path_mktemp\f[R] without sourcing the library.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.PP
\f[V]libstring.sh\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Commands assist with string processing.
.IP \[bu] 2
Includes commands to safely and easily trim, truncate, substitute
values, and more.
.SS ENVIRONMENT
.PP
Several variables affect the libraries, some of these should be set to
specific values for the libraries to work as intended[1], while others
determine library configuration.
.SS SHELL CONFIGURATION
.SS LOCALE
.PP
TL;DR:
.IP \[bu] 2
The expression \f[V]${LC_ALL:-${LC_CTYPE:-${LANG:-POSIX}}}\f[R]
\f[I]SHOULD\f[R] evaluate to \f[V]POSIX\f[R] or \f[V]C\f[R]; other
values \f[I]MAY\f[R] work, but are \f[I]NOT\f[R] generally supported;
.IP \[bu] 2
The locale of the currently active shell can \f[B]not\f[R] be changed -
the locale in effect for a shell is \f[B]always\f[R] the locale in
effect when the shell was invoked;
.IP \[bu] 2
Using the correct locale for the data being processed is essential -
multi- byte characters can \f[B]not\f[R] be processed correctly with
many operations \f[I]unless\f[R] a suitable locale is currently in
effect.
.IP \[bu] 2
Some libraries (e.g.\ \f[V]libstring.sh\f[R]) make significant attempts
to be correct using \f[I]any\f[R] locale, but this relies on the
underlying tools supporting the specific locale - such support varies by
platform and is impossible to test for portably.
.PP
Details:
.PP
All libraries assume the \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] defined default environment
is in effect.
.PP
\f[B]Much of the \f[BI]POSIX.1\f[B] standard is \f[BI]only\f[B] defined
for this environment.\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
All libraries are locale agnostic to the extent possible, however much
of the standard is \f[I]only\f[R] defined when the locale is the
\f[I]POSIX\f[R] locale - implementations can (and do) provide different
behavior with other locales.
.IP \[bu] 2
In most cases any locale should work, and in cases where there are known
issues with implementation behavior outside the \f[I]POSIX\f[R] locale,
libraries may be able to set the locale directly to avoid issues (though
this is not always possible).
.IP \[bu] 2
There are several standard defined locale variables, namely:
\f[V]LANG\f[R], \f[V]LC_COLLATE\f[R], \f[V]LC_CTYPE\f[R],
\f[V]LC_MESSAGES\f[R], \f[V]LC_MONETARY\f[R], \f[V]LC_NUMERIC\f[R],
\f[V]LC_TIME\f[R], and \f[V]LC_ALL\f[R].
The variable \f[V]LANG\f[R] provides a default for any unset variables,
while \f[V]LC_ALL\f[R] overrides all other variables.
Typically \f[V]LC_CTYPE\f[R] is the variable that controls if utilities
behave according to the standard, while other variables ensure
processing is in accordance with locale settings
(e.g.\ \f[V]LC_MESSAGES\f[R] may change the language used for error
messages).[2]
.IP \[bu] 2
Although the \f[I]POSIX\f[R] locale does not recognize multi-byte
characters the standard requires text in the \f[I]POSIX\f[R] locale is
processed as bytes so multi-byte characters can be processed correctly
in many cases, however some operations \f[I]will\f[R] behave in
unexpected ways.
.IP \[bu] 2
Many systems now provide UTF-8 versions of \f[V]POSIX\f[R] and
\f[V]C\f[R] - note that these are \f[I]NOT\f[R] the \f[I]POSIX\f[R]
locale, although tools may behave as if they are.[3]
.IP \[bu] 2
The locale in use affects which tools are used for some operations.[4]
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]IFS\f[R]
.PP
TL;DR:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]SHOULD\f[R] be the standard defined default of
\f[V]<space><tab><newline>\f[R]; other values \f[I]MAY\f[R] work, but
are \f[I]NOT\f[R] supported.
.PP
Details:
.IP \[bu] 2
All libraries are agnostic to the value of \f[V]IFS\f[R] the extent
possible, however \f[V]IFS\f[R] plays a role in a significant number of
fundamental shell operations, and, as such, can cause bugs that may be
difficult to understand.
.IP \[bu] 2
Historically, shell support of \f[V]IFS\f[R] has been somewhat
inconsistent, although more recently the situation has improved.
Sadly, there remain edge cases where behavior of operations that rely on
\f[V]IFS\f[R] is different between shells.
.IP \[bu] 2
No library sets the value of \f[V]IFS\f[R].
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]POSIXLY_CORRECT\f[R]
.PP
TL;DR:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]SHOULD\f[R] be the set.
.PP
Details:
.IP \[bu] 2
In addition to the current locale, \f[I]GNU\f[R]
tools (https://www.gnu.org/prep/standards/html_node/Non_002dGNU-Standards.html)
may also check if this variable is set - if so they will try to match
the standard more closely than otherwise would be the case.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]GNU\f[R] tools are near omnipresent - available for almost any
conceivable platform and often installed by default.
Given this, and that other implementations may also use this variable,
it is prudent to set this.
.SS LIBRARY CONFIGURATION
.PP
A number of environment variables affect the functionality of each of
the libraries.
These include both variables that instruct the library to work-around
specific platform issues, and variables that convey user preferences.
(See also the information on compatibility.)
.PP
Where it is possible, platform specific issues are detected
automatically, with the associated variables providing a way to force
enabling or disabling specific work-arounds if necessary.
\f[I]Automatic detection should always be preferred\f[R] - this detects
use cases that are actually problematic and not more general issues.
.SS LIBRARY CONFIGURATION: VARIABLE CATEGORIES
.PP
Each configuration variable belongs to a specific \f[I]CLASS\f[R]:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]CONSTANT\f[R] - a configuration option that is read \f[I]only
once\f[R] when the library is first sourced and must not be set after
this point (the \f[V]readonly\f[R] command may be used to enforce this).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]VARIABLE\f[R] - a configuration option that can be modified at any
point and \f[I]may\f[R] affect the next command.
.PP
Additionally, each configuration variable has to a specific
\f[I]TYPE\f[R]:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]TEXT\f[R] - has a value that is arbitrary text with constraints
defined by each specific variable.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]FLAG\f[R] - enables or disables specific functionality.
The value \f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]<zero>\f[R]) turns a flag \f[I]OFF\f[R],
while \f[B]\f[BI]any\f[B]\f[R] other text will turn a flag \f[I]ON\f[R],
EXCEPT for flags where automatic detection is applicable where the value
\f[V]A\f[R] is special and forces the use of automatic detection.
In some cases automatic detection can be delayed until specific
functionality is required, in these cases the value \f[V]D\f[R] can be
used to forced delayed automatic detection - if delayed detection is not
supported for a flag, \f[V]D\f[R] and \f[V]A\f[R] are equivalent.[5]
\f[I]A flag that is unset \f[BI]or\f[I] \f[R]set but null\f[I]
\f[R](i.e.\ empty) will use an appropriate default value._
.PP
Configuration variables are never modified by a library.
.PP
Many configuration variables can be set for all libraries with a single,
suite wide variable.
Where such a suite wide variable is available a library specific
variable is always available in addition and has precedence.
(Not all libraries use all suite wide settings.)
.PP
Suite wide variables are listed in the following sections (with defaults
highlighted).
.SS LIBRARY CONFIGURATION: USER PREFERENCE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_QUIET_ERRORS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]VARIABLE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[I]OFF\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
[Enable]/Disable library error message output.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: error messages will be written to \f[V]STDERR\f[R] as:
\f[V][<IDENTIFIER>]: ERROR: <MESSAGE>\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: library error messages will be suppressed.
.IP \[bu] 2
Each library also stores the most recent error message in a library
specific variable, which is unaffected by this flag.
.IP \[bu] 2
Unless otherwise stated, both the library versions of this option and
the suite version can be modified between command invocations and should
affect the next command.
.IP \[bu] 2
Does not affect errors from non-library commands, which \f[I]may\f[R]
still produce output.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_FATAL_ERRORS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]VARIABLE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[I]OFF\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Enable/[Disable] causing library errors to terminate the current
(sub-)shell.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: errors stop any further processing, and cause a non-zero
exit status, but do not cause an exception.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: any library error will cause an \[lq]unset variable\[rq]
shell exception using the
\f[V]${parameter:?[word]}\f[R] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/utilities/V3_chap02.html#tag_18_06_02)
parameter expansion, where \f[V]word\f[R] is set to an error message
that \f[I]should\f[R] be displayed by the shell (this message is NOT
suppressed by \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_QUIET_ERRORS\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
Unless otherwise stated, both the library versions of this option and
the suite version can be modified between command invocations and should
affect the next command.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS LIBRARY CONFIGURATION: PORTABILITY
.PP
\f[B]Where possible these are automatically detected - unless there is a
problem\f[R] \f[B]it is \f[BI]HIGHLY\f[B] recommended these are not set
manually.\f[R][6]
.PP
\f[I]Incorrectly setting these variables \f[BI]will\f[I] cause errors in
many cases\f[R] \f[I](including possible data corruption), while in
other cases may result\f[R] \f[I]in lost performance.\f[R]
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_NO_Z_SHELL_SETOPT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
[Disable]/Enable using \f[V]setopt\f[R] in \f[I]Z Shell\f[R] to ensure
\f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] like behavior.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: Use \f[V]setopt\f[R] to set the appropriate options.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: Don\[cq]t use \f[V]setopt\f[R], even in \f[I]Z Shell\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Automatically enabled if \f[I]Z Shell\f[R] is detected.
.IP \[bu] 2
Any use of \f[V]setopt\f[R] is scoped as tightly as possible and should
not affect other commands.
.IP \[bu] 2
If \f[I]Z Shell\f[R] is used and the current environment has already
been set to be \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] compliant, forcing this \f[I]ON\f[R]
may improve performance.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]Z Shell\f[R] has some defaults that cause non-standard behavior,
however also provides \f[V]setopt\f[R] which can be tightly scoped to
set options when required without impacting other platforms.[7]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]WARNING:\f[R] setting this variable incorrectly \f[I]will\f[R]
cause errors.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_NO_MULTIDIGIT_PARAMETER\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
[Disable]/Enable using only single digit shell parameters,
i.e.\ \f[V]$0\f[R] to \f[V]$9\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: Use multi-digit shell parameters.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: Use only single-digit shell parameters.
.IP \[bu] 2
Multi-digit parameters are faster but may not be supported by all
implementations.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_NO_SHIFT_N\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
[Disable]/Enable using only \f[V]shift\f[R] and not \f[V]shift N\f[R]
for multiple parameters.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: Use \f[V]shift N\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: Use only \f[V]shift\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Multi-parameter \f[V]shift\f[R] is faster but may not be supported by
all implementations.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_SHELL_SUPPORTS_MBC\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
Disable/[Enable] support for multi-byte character processing within the
shell itself (e.g.\ via wildcards).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: use fallback code for operations affected.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: use internal shell operations.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]The locale a shell uses is set at invocation and can \f[BI]not\f[I]
be changed for\f[R] \f[I]a running shell.\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default is to run tests for the current shell when a library is sourced
to determine if such support is present.
.IP \[bu] 2
To support multi-byte characters, requires a shell is invoked using a
locale that defines such characters (i.e.\ \f[B]not\f[R] the
\f[I]POSIX\f[R] locale) \f[B]and\f[R] the shell must support them.
.IP \[bu] 2
Support for shell string processing of multi-byte characters is far from
universal, outside the \f[I]POSIX\f[R] locale implementations often
continue to use byte processing rather than character processing - in
some cases implementations provide partial support, which can be
confusing.
.IP \[bu] 2
Often lack of support for multi-byte characters is not an issue, however
in certain circumstances it can cause problems, including corrupting
data.
.IP \[bu] 2
Note that some operations are \f[B]always\f[R] processed as bytes (not
characters) regardless of locale - workarounds for these operations are
used based solely on the current locale and are not affected by this
flag.
.IP \[bu] 2
The costs of running tests are small, but may be measurable for short
scripts, setting this variable avoids these costs.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]NOTE:\f[R] no implementation supports multi-byte characters if
invoked in the \f[I]POSIX\f[R] locale.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]WARNING:\f[R] setting this variable incorrectly \f[I]may\f[R] lead
to errors and data corruption.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]WARNING:\f[R] fallback implementations may be significantly slower.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]WARNING:\f[R] set the current locale \f[I]before\f[R] sourcing a
library \f[B]will\f[R] break automatic testing.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_SHELL_SUPPORTS_PORTABLE_GLOB\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
Disable/[Enable] glob/wildcard pattern matching even if the pattern
contains known problematic characters.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: use fallback code for patterns that contain problem
characters.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: use shell pattern matching.
.IP \[bu] 2
Default is to run tests for the current shell when a library is sourced
to determine if the current shell supports these as expected or not.
.IP \[bu] 2
Some implementations do not treat all characters in patterns in the way
that is expected - e.g.\ \f[V]\[rs]\f[R] (\f[V]<backslash>\f[R]) is an
escape character for \f[I]most\f[R] implementations, but always literal
for others.
.IP \[bu] 2
The costs of running tests are small, but may be measurable for short
scripts, setting this variable avoids these costs.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]WARNING:\f[R] setting this variable incorrectly \f[I]may\f[R] lead
to errors and data corruption.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]WARNING:\f[R] fallback implementations may be significantly slower.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_NO_PARAM_EXPANSION_CHAR_CLASS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
Disable/[Enable] using character classes of the form \f[V][:alpha:]\f[R]
in \[lq]Parameter
Expansion\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/utilities/V3_chap02.html#tag_18_06_02).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: use fallback code for operations affected.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: use character classes.
.IP \[bu] 2
Using character classes allows some operations to be completed within
the shell itself, rather than requiring external commands, but these
classes are not always supported for this.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]WARNING:\f[R] fallback implementations may be significantly slower.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_NO_CASE_CHAR_CLASS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
Disable/[Enable] using character classes of the form \f[V][:alpha:]\f[R]
in \f[V]case\f[R] pattern matching expression.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: use fallback code for operations affected.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: use character classes.
.IP \[bu] 2
Using character classes allows some operations to be completed within
the shell itself, rather than requiring external commands, but these
classes are not always supported for this.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]WARNING:\f[R] fallback implementations may be significantly slower.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_NO_DEV_NULL\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
[Disable]/Enable using alternatives to \f[V]/dev/null\f[R] as a
redirection source/target (e.g.\ for output suppression).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: Use \f[V]/dev/null\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: Use an alternative to \f[V]/dev/null\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Using \f[V]/dev/null\f[R] as a redirection target is a common idiom, but
not always possible (e.g.\ restricted shells generally forbid this).
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_NO_GREP_E\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
[Disable]/Enable using the non-standard \f[V]egrep\f[R] instead of
\f[V]grep -E\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: Use \f[V]grep -E\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: Use \f[V]egrep\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
While \f[V]grep -E\f[R] is standard, it is not always supported -
implementations that do not support it often provide the non-standard
\f[V]egrep\f[R] instead.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]WARNING:\f[R] no check is made that \f[V]egrep\f[R] exists: if
\f[V]grep -E\f[R] is not available \f[V]egrep\f[R] is assumed to be
present.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_NO_GREP_F\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
[Disable]/Enable using the non-standard \f[V]fgrep\f[R] instead of
\f[V]grep -F\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: Use \f[V]grep -F\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: Use \f[V]fgrep\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
While \f[V]grep -F\f[R] is standard, it is not always supported -
implementations that do not support it usually provide the non-standard
\f[V]fgrep\f[R] instead.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]WARNING:\f[R] no check is made that \f[V]fgrep\f[R] exists: if
\f[V]grep -F\f[R] is not available \f[V]fgrep\f[R] is assumed to be
present.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_TR_SUPPORTS_MBC\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic> (delayed)
.IP \[bu] 2
Disable/[Enable] support for multi-byte character processing with
\f[V]tr\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: use fallback code for operations affected.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: use \f[V]tr\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Default is to run tests when \f[V]tr\f[R] is needed to determine if such
support is present.
.IP \[bu] 2
Some common implementations of \f[V]tr\f[R] lack multi-byte character
support, regardless of the locale in use.
This includes \f[I]GNU\f[R] \f[V]tr\f[R].
Although such implementations should not corrupt data, they will also
not process multi- byte characters correctly.
(e.g.\ the common \f[V]tr \[aq][:upper:]\[aq] \[aq][:lower:]\[aq]\f[R]
fails to work as expected).
.IP \[bu] 2
The costs of running tests are small, but may be measurable for short
scripts, setting this variable avoids these costs.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]WARNING:\f[R] fallback implementations may be significantly slower.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]WARNING:\f[R] setting this when not required may cause issues if
fallback utilities do not support multi-byte character processing
themselves.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_NO_TR_CASE_CLASS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
Disable/[Enable] using character classes of the form \f[V][:alpha:]\f[R]
with \f[V]tr\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: use fallback code for operations affected.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: use character classes.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]tr\f[R] is one of the fastest commands available for certain tasks,
but if it does not support character classes, alternatives may be
required.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]WARNING:\f[R] fallback implementations may be significantly slower.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_PREFER_HEXDUMP\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
Enable/[Disable] using \f[V]hexdump\f[R] in preference to \f[V]od\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: use \f[V]od\f[R] whenever possible.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: use \f[V]hexdump\f[R] even if \f[V]od\f[R] is available.
.IP \[bu] 2
Has no effect unless both \f[V]od\f[R] and \f[V]hexdump\f[R] are
available.
.IP \[bu] 2
Some systems lack \f[V]od\f[R], but provide \f[V]hexdump\f[R] for the
same purpose, while many provide both.
This flag allows the use of either \f[V]od\f[R] or \f[V]hexdump\f[R] to
be preferred.
.IP \[bu] 2
The default is to use \f[V]od\f[R] if available and \f[V]hexdump\f[R]
otherwise.
If neither command is available different (situation dependent) methods
for generating data are used.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]Provided for cases where \f[VI]hexdump\f[I] is more capable than
\f[VI]od\f[I]\f[R]
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_RANDOM_SOURCE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]TEXT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[V]/dev/urandom\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Specify a source for random data.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]MUST\f[R] be either the special value \f[V]awk\f[R] or a path to
use as a source for random data.
.IP \[bu] 2
If specified as a path, the path \f[I]MUST\f[R] be readable, and
\f[I]MUST\f[R] behave like \f[V]/dev/urandom\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
If specified as \f[V]awk\f[R], random data is generated by \f[V]awk\f[R]
- \f[B]this is insecure\f[R] as the data generated is of poor quality
and likely to be easy to guess.
.IP \[bu] 2
Has no effect if neither \f[V]od\f[R] nor \f[V]hexdump\f[R] is
available.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]NOTE:\f[R] The commonly present \f[V]/dev/random\f[R] and
\f[V]/dev/urandom\f[R] are not standard, while they can be used
similarly they do not behave the same way in all circumstances in all
implementations.
Care should be taken to ensure they work as expected on any platform
they will be used.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]WARNING:\f[R] \f[I]both\f[R] \f[V]/dev/random\f[R] \f[B]and\f[R]
\f[V]/dev/urandom\f[R] \f[B]can\f[R] block - when this may occur varies
by system, but care must be taken when using these to avoid stalls.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_NO_AWK_ARGV\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]VARIABLE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[I]OFF\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Disable/[Enable] using \f[V]ARGV\f[R] within \f[V]awk\f[R] - enabling
gives significantly better performance, but is subject to some
limitations.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: Use \f[V]ARGV\f[R] within \f[V]awk\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: Avoid \f[V]ARGV\f[R] within \f[V]awk\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
When \f[I]OFF\f[R] \f[V]ARGV\f[R] will be used whenever appropriate, if
this fails (likely due one of the limitations), the code for the
\f[I]OFF\f[R] condition will be used to get the required results.
This comes with a small cost as the \f[V]ARGV\f[R] code must first be
run and fail (although this should be relatively fast, it does have an
impact).
.IP \[bu] 2
This is \f[B]not\f[R] autodetected as the point is \f[I]not\f[R] to test
if \f[V]ARGV\f[R] is available (it is assumed to be), but if it should
be used for performance reasons.
There is no real way to test this it will be system and data specific.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS LIBRARY INFORMATION
.PP
Each library provides a number of variables that are set by the library
to convey information outside of command invocation.
.PP
These variables must not be set by external commands except if this is
\f[I]explicitly\f[R] permitted.
Variables may use the \f[V]readonly\f[R] command to enforce this.
.PP
Along with any library only information variables, every library also
provides a version of some standard variables:
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_VERSION_MAJOR\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
A whole number >= 1.
.IP \[bu] 2
Incremented when there are significant changes, or any changes break
compatibility with previous library versions.
.IP \[bu] 2
Follows Semantic Versioning v2.0.0 (https://semver.org/).
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_VERSION_MINOR\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
A whole number >= 0.
.IP \[bu] 2
Incremented for significant changes that do not break compatibility with
previous versions.
.IP \[bu] 2
Reset to 0 when \f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_VERSION_MAJOR\f[R] changes.
.IP \[bu] 2
Follows Semantic Versioning v2.0.0 (https://semver.org/).
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_VERSION_PATCH\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
A whole number >= 0.
.IP \[bu] 2
Incremented for minor revisions or bugfixes.
.IP \[bu] 2
Reset to 0 when \f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_VERSION_MINOR\f[R] changes.
.IP \[bu] 2
Follows Semantic Versioning v2.0.0 (https://semver.org/).
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_VERSION_RELEASE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
A string indicating a pre-release version.
.IP \[bu] 2
Always null for full-release versions.
.IP \[bu] 2
Possible values include \f[V]alpha\f[R], \f[V]beta\f[R], \f[V]rc\f[R],
etc, (a numerical suffix may also be appended).
.IP \[bu] 2
Follows Semantic Versioning v2.0.0 (https://semver.org/).
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_VERSION_FULL\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Full (numerical) version combining \f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_VERSION_MAJOR\f[R],
\f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_VERSION_MINOR\f[R], and
\f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_VERSION_PATCH\f[R] as a single value.
.IP \[bu] 2
Can be used in numerical comparisons.
.IP \[bu] 2
Format is \f[V]MNNNPPP\f[R] where, \f[V]M\f[R] is the \f[V]MAJOR\f[R]
version, \f[V]NNN\f[R] is the \f[V]MINOR\f[R] version (3 digit, zero
padded), and \f[V]PPP\f[R] is the \f[V]PATCH\f[R] version (3 digit, zero
padded).
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_VERSION\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Full version combining \f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_VERSION_MAJOR\f[R],
\f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_VERSION_MINOR\f[R],
\f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_VERSION_PATCH\f[R], and
\f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_VERSION_RELEASE\f[R] as a formatted string.
.IP \[bu] 2
Format is
\f[V]BetterScripts \[aq]<library>\[aq] vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH[-RELEASE]\f[R].
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_LAST_ERROR\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Stores the error message of the \f[I]most recent\f[R] library error.
.IP \[bu] 2
ONLY valid immediately following a command from the appropriate library
for which the exit status is not a success code.
.IP \[bu] 2
Valid even when error output is suppressed.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_SOURCED\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Set (and non-null) once the library has been sourced.
.IP \[bu] 2
Dependant scripts can query if this variable is set to determine if a
specific library has been sourced.
.IP \[bu] 2
Also serves as a guard to avoid errors caused by sourcing a library
multiple times.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS DEBUG VARIABLES
.PP
Each library supports limited debug information that can be controlled
using environment variables.
Library specific variables take precedence.
The widely used \f[V]DEBUG\f[R] environment variable provides a default
if all other variables are unset.
.PP
In particular, debug output helps determine which code path is used for
those commands that vary implementation depending on the capabilities of
the current platform.
(In some cases these paths are determined dynamically.)
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_DEBUG\f[R] and \f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_DEBUG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]VARIABLE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[I]OFF\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Enable/[Disable] library debug message output.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: no debug messages will be displayed.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: debug messages will be displayed.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_DEBUG\f[R] defaults to the expression
\f[V]${BS_DEBUG:-${DEBUG:-0}}\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Also enables \f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_CONFIG_DEBUG\f[R].
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_CONFIG_DEBUG\f[R] and \f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_CONFIG_DEBUG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]VARIABLE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[I]OFF\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Enable/[Disable] library debug message output for configuration only.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: no debug messages will be displayed.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: debug messages will be displayed.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_CONFIG_DEBUG\f[R] defaults to the expression
\f[V]${BS_CONFIG_DEBUG:-${BS_<LIBRARY>_DEBUG:-0}}\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Configuration debug is only displayed on sourcing.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_DEBUG_FD\f[R] and \f[V]BS_<LIBRARY>_DEBUG_FD\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]TEXT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]VARIABLE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[V]2\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Set a target for debug messages.
.IP \[bu] 2
If the target is a single digit \f[I]or\f[R] is preceded by an
\f[V]&\f[R] (\f[V]<ampersand>\f[R]) output is redirected using
\f[V]>&\f[R] (i.e.\ it is treated as a file descriptor); if the target
is \f[I]not\f[R] a single digit \f[I]or\f[R] is preceded by a
\f[V]>\f[R] (\f[V]<greater-than-sign>\f[R]) output is redirected using
\f[V]>>\f[R] (i.e.\ it is treated as a file).
Any \f[V]&\f[R] (\f[V]<ampersand>\f[R]) or \f[V]>\f[R]
(\f[V]<greater-than-sign>\f[R]) prefix is removed before use.
.IP \[bu] 2
In some cases \f[V]STDERR\f[R] is suppressed by commands - unless
another target is specified this may lead to some debug messages also
being suppressed.
.IP \[bu] 2
If specified as a file descriptor other than \f[V]1\f[R] or \f[V]2\f[R]
the descriptor MUST be opened before it is used.
This can be accomplished in multiple ways, the simplest of which is to
use something similar to \f[V]{ ...; } N>&1\f[R] where \f[V]N\f[R] is
the relevant file descriptor and any code that uses the descriptor is
enclosed within the braces.
Alternatively the \f[V]exec\f[R] command can be used to similar effect.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS ADDITIONAL VARIABLES
.SS \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_PATH\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]SHOULD\f[R] be set to the location of the \f[V]shtoolkit\f[R]
files.
.IP \[bu] 2
Multiple paths may be specified - formatted like the standard variable
\f[V]PATH\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Useful for users if libraries are not installed in a location that is
available in \f[V]PATH\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Currently used only by test helper scripts.
.SS VERSIONS
.PP
As each \f[V]shtoolkit\f[R] library is designed to be independent,
versioning is on a per-library basis.
.PP
Each release of \f[V]shtoolkit\f[R] as a whole is given a version of the
form \f[V]vYY.N\f[R] where \f[V]YY\f[R] is the year of the release and
\f[V]N\f[R] is an integer \f[V]>=1\f[R] and is incremented for each
release within a given year.
.PP
The \f[V]shtoolkit\f[R] release version is for reference only and is not
present in code and can not be quired.
Version checks should be made against individual library versions.
.SS STANDARDS
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]POSIX.1-2008\f[R] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition)
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Also known as:
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]The Open Group Base Specifications Issue 7\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]IEEE Std 1003.1-2008\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]The Single UNIX Specification Version 4 (SUSv4)\f[R]
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
There are several more recent versions of the standard:
2013 (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2013edition),
2016 (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2016edition),
2018 (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2018edition), and
2024 (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799.2024edition) -
while these alter the standard, most of the \f[I]2008\f[R] version still
applies.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
FreeBSD SYSEXITS(3) (https://www.freebsd.org/cgi/man.cgi?sysexits(3))
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Although not a standard, the values specified by \f[I]SYSEXITS\f[R] are
widely used and are the only common exit codes generally available.
.IP \[bu] 2
Libraries use these values wherever possible, however other exit codes
may occur:
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Values returned by external commands are propagated where possible and
appropriate.
.IP \[bu] 2
As per \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] the value \f[V]1\f[R] if used for
\f[V]false\f[R] for commands that require reporting a non-success,
non-error exit status.
.RE
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
Semantic Versioning v2.0.0 (https://semver.org/)
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Each library has its own version number, each of which complies with
Semantic Versioning v2.0.0.
.IP \[bu] 2
Some libraries may provide version numbers for additional purposes,
these also follow Semantic Versioning v2.0.0, but may not include all
elements.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
Inclusive Naming Initiative (https://inclusivenaming.org/).
.SS NOTES
.SS INSTALLATION
.PP
The provided Makefile has targets that allow for installation of both
libraries and documentation in configurable locations (by default
libraries are installed in \f[V]/usr/local/bin\f[R], \f[V]Markdown\f[R]
documentation in \f[V]/usr/local/share/doc\f[R] and \f[V]man\f[R] page
documentation in the appropriate \f[V]/usr/local/share/man\f[R]
directory for the documentation category - note that these are not
\f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] specified).
.PP
Most of the libraries are intended to be sourced by other scripts using
the \f[V].\f[R] (aka dot)
command (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/utilities/V3_chap02.html#dot),
for which the standard says:
.RS
.PP
If file does not contain a <slash>, the shell shall use the search path
specified by PATH to find the directory containing file.
Unlike normal command search, however, the file searched for by the dot
utility need not be executable.
.RE
.PP
As such libraries are installed as non-executable unless direct
invocation is supported for a specific library.
.PP
More information about installation is available by invoking the
\f[V]help\f[R] target from the Makefile, i.e.\ \f[V]make help\f[R].
.SS DOCUMENTATION
.PP
Up-to-date versions of documentation for each library is always present
in the main repository (https://github.com/BetterScripts/shtoolkit) in
both
\f[V]Markdown\f[R] (https://daringfireball.net/projects/markdown/syntax)
and \f[V]man\f[R] page (https://wikipedia.org/wiki/Man_page) formats.
.PP
Much of this documentation is generated from other files within the
suite, with \f[V]Markdown\f[R] documentation for libraries being
generated from comments in the libraries themselves, while \f[V]man\f[R]
page documentation is generated from \f[V]Markdown\f[R] documentation
for both common and library documentation.
.PP
All \f[V]Markdown\f[R] documentation aims to be compatible with the
original
\f[V]Markdown\f[R] (https://daringfireball.net/projects/markdown/syntax)
specification, with reference to
\f[V]CommonMark\f[R] (https://commonmark.org/) to resolve any
ambiguities.
Although an extension to the original standard, footnotes are used
throughout the \f[V]Markdown\f[R] documentation as they are highly
useful, widely supported, and acceptably rendered by \f[V]Markdown\f[R]
flavors that do not support them.
.PP
Documentation can be regenerated using the suite provided Makefile.
.SS COMMAND ARGUMENTS
.PP
Library commands document arguments with a tag indicating argument
usage:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]in\f[R]: provides data \f[B]TO\f[R] the command.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]out\f[R]: receives data \f[B]FROM\f[R] the command.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]in/out\f[R]: provides data \f[B]TO\f[R] \f[I]AND\f[R] receives data
\f[B]FROM\f[R] the command.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ref\f[R]: an additional tag indicating the argument is passed by
\f[B]NAME\f[R] instead of \f[B]VALUE\f[R].
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
For a typical \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] variable this means omitting the
\f[V]$\f[R] from the name when passing it to the command, i.e.\ instead
of passing \f[V]$Variable\f[R] (or \f[V]${Variable}\f[R]) use
\f[V]Variable\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Only \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] compliant names are permitted.
Due to the security considerations of using \f[V]eval\f[R] with
arbitrary text \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] names are enforced for all variable
names; providing a non-standard name will cause an error (even if the
name is supported by the current shell).
.IP \[bu] 2
Variables passed by name are \f[I]GLOBAL\f[R] variables.
The use of \f[V]local\f[R] variables (as supported by many shells) will
\f[B]not\f[R] work as expected.
(\f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] has no concept of \f[V]local\f[R] variables.)[8]
.RE
.PP
Command options, where available, can be specified as \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R]
style single character options following a single \f[V]-\f[R]
(\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]) character, and as \f[I]GNU\f[R] style long options
following a two \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]) characters.
.PP
Unless otherwise specified:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] style single character options can be combined into a
single multi- character option;
.IP \[bu] 2
option arguments can be specified as immediately following the
corresponding option, as a suffix to a single character option, or
appended to a multi- character option using an \f[V]=\f[R]
(\f[V]<equals>\f[R]) character.
.PP
Options \f[I]MUST\f[R] precede operands.
.PP
The special argument \f[V]--\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen><hyphen>\f[R]) can be
used to disable option processing and treat all subsequent arguments as
operands.
.SS COMPATIBILITY
.PP
All of \f[V]shtoolkit\f[R] is designed to be supported in any
environment that is compatible with the \[lq]command interpretation
services and common utility programs\[rq] as defined in the \[lq]Shell
and
Utilities\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/idx/xcu.html)
volume of the
\f[I]POSIX.1-2008\f[R] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition)
standard.
.PP
\f[I]The number of environments that are at least partially POSIX.1
compliant is enormous - even if it were possible to test all of them,
access to many is difficult as they are tied to proprietary/specialist
systems.
Therefore, much of the compatibility work for the Suite is based on
resources such as \[lq]autoconf: Portable Shell
Programming\[rq] (https://www.gnu.org/savannah-checkouts/gnu/autoconf/manual/html_node/Portable-Shell.html)).
While such resources are incredibly useful, they often omit details such
as the specific platforms for which problems occur, or even dates for
when the problem was discovered or last seen.
The result is that it is highly likely some of the workarounds
implemented are unnecessary, and other necessary workarounds have been
omitted.\f[R][9]
.PP
It is likely that any \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] like environment that supports
\[lq]Parameter
Expansion\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/utilities/V3_chap02.html#tag_18_06_02),
modern style \[lq]Command
Substitution\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/utilities/V3_chap02.html#tag_18_06_03)
(i.e.\ \f[V]$(command)\f[R]) and \[lq]Arithmetic
Expansion\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/utilities/V3_chap02.html#tag_18_06_04)
will at least partially support \f[V]shtoolkit\f[R][10].
.PP
Additionally:
.IP \[bu] 2
The \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] standard has remained relative consistent between
versions (as relates to functionality required by \f[V]shtoolkit\f[R]),
although the
\f[I]POSIX.1-2008\f[R] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition)
version of the standard is the reference version used for creating
\f[V]shtoolkit\f[R] it is likely that earlier versions will also be
supported.
.IP \[bu] 2
Non-compliant shells and utilities may be supported by specific
libraries, or specific commands within those libraries.
.IP \[bu] 2
A shell and/or utilities which are \f[I]not\f[R] supported \f[I]may\f[R]
still be able to make use of \f[V]shtoolkit\f[R] - any such tool is
termed \f[I]compatible\f[R].
The difference between shells and utilities which are
\f[I]supported\f[R] and those which are \f[I]compatible\f[R] is that any
erroneous behavior specific to the latter is not technically a bug and
unlikely to be addressed.
.IP \[bu] 2
Commands are designed to be \f[I]functionally equivalent\f[R] regardless
of the value of any of the standard specified shell options
(e.g.\ \f[V]errexit\f[R], \f[V]nounset\f[R], etc).
.IP \[bu] 2
Where a shell or utility is known to deviate from the functionality
required by a library a work-around may be provided \f[I]if\f[R] it is
relatively simple, performant, and can be scoped to only affect library
commands.
.IP \[bu] 2
Some common, but non-standard functionality is supported, for example,
\[lq]restricted\[rq] shells.
.IP \[bu] 2
Tests for suite libraries are provided along with a test harness in
which they are run.
These are not primarily intended to determine platform support, but are
designed more for regression testing.
Additionally, the test harness, while \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] compliant, it
currently require sa more capable platform than that of individual
libraries.
That said, if tests run successfully for a specific platform it is
likely the platform will be fully supported.
.RS
.PP
All libraries have been tested in multiple operating systems including
\f[I]Ubuntu\f[R], \f[I]Oracle Solaris\f[R], \f[I]FreeBSD\f[R],
\f[I]OpenBSD\f[R], and \f[I]Windows Subsystem for Linux\f[R].
.PP
Multiple implementations of \[lq]Shells and Utilities\[rq] have also
been tested including: \f[V]sh\f[R][11], \f[V]bash\f[R], \f[V]bosh\f[R],
\f[V]busybox\f[R], \f[V]dash\f[R], \f[V]ksh88\f[R], \f[V]ksh93\f[R],
\f[V]mksh\f[R], \f[V]modernish\f[R], \f[V]osh\f[R], \f[V]oksh\f[R],
\f[V]pdksh\f[R], \f[V]posh\f[R], \f[V]yash\f[R], and \f[V]zsh\f[R]
(including \[lq]restricted\[rq] versions of these shells where known to
exist) - all shells are tested in \[lq]default\[rq] mode along with any
\f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] compatibility mode.
.PP
Various implementations of utilities have also been tested.
.RE
.PP
\f[I]Note that the presence of a shell in the list of those tested does
\f[BI]not\f[I] imply a shell is supported for all libraries and in all
use cases.\f[R]
.SS COMPATIBILITY: SHELLS
.PP
There are a significant number of implementations of \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R]
compliant shells, and each of these has it\[cq]s own quirks, bugs, and
interpretation of the standard.
.PP
While most shells are possible to coax into working as expected (even
when they do not do so by default), there are some shells which are
problematic in one way or another (or have versions known to be).
Those included in this section are those that benefit from some remarks
- this is in no way a comment on these particular implementations.
.PP
Work to better accommodate problematic shells and shells not yet tested
remains ongoing.
.PP
Legacy shells that do not support things like modern command
substitution syntax, arithmetic expansion, and parameter expansion
remain unsupported.
.SS \f[V]busybox\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]busybox\f[R] is widely available and fully supported, however, not
all distributions of \f[V]busybox\f[R] are created equal and some
commonly available versions are significantly less capable than others.
.PP
By design \f[V]busybox\f[R] is enormously customizable at build time and
it is not uncommon for binary distributions to omit a lot of the
optional code.
Unfortunately there appears to be no way to determine what configuration
was used to build a specific version and so it is not easy to use any
potential workarounds that might help, and so some things may fail with
some versions of \f[V]busybox\f[R] and not others.
.PP
\f[I]Tests on builds using \f[VI]defconfig\f[I] work as expected (in
line with other shells).\f[R]
.PP
Additionally \f[B]\f[VB]v1.36\f[B]\f[R] of \f[V]busybox\f[R] is known to
have numerous issues related to \f[V]awk\f[R] which result in
segmentation faults in a number of cases.
These issues are fixed as of \f[V]v1.37\f[R].
.PP
For these reasons the test harness has an option \f[V]--no-busybox\f[R]
which removes \f[V]busybox\f[R] from the list of known shells when
running tests - if tests fail \f[I]only\f[R] in \f[V]busybox\f[R] these
are likely false positives and can often safely be ignored.
.SS \f[V]ksh93\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]ksh93\f[R] version \f[V]Version AJM 93u+\f[R] has issues with the
test harness that cause a number of false negatives.
These seem to be related to problems capturing \f[V]STDERR\f[R] via
command substitution in certain circumstances.
A number of tests are therefore skipped with this version of this shell
as they do not work.
.PP
\f[I]These failures are with the test harness only.\f[R] They do not
affect the usability of this version of this shell with the libraries
themselves.
.SS \f[V]nsh\f[R]
.PP
Currently the only known shell \f[B]not\f[R] to be automatically used by
the test harness and, currently, can not be tested as it does not
correctly process command line arguments with \f[V]-c\f[R].
.SS COMPATIBILITY: BINARIES
.PP
A number of \[lq]binaries\[rq] are provided as alternative methods of
accessing specific functionality from the libraries.
Each of these is simply a wrapper script that sources the relevant
library and invokes a single function within the library with the given
arguments.
.PP
\f[I]These scripts provide \f[BI]no\f[I] additional functionality beyond
that available in\f[R] \f[I]the libraries themselves.\f[R]
.PP
Importantly, though, these scripts will execute using \f[V]sh\f[R] for
the current system - on systems where \f[V]sh\f[R] is a unique shell
that is less capable than required, these commands \f[B]will
fail\f[R].[12]
.SS COMPATIBILITY: EXTERNAL COMMANDS
.PP
Every library provided relies on many of the standard specified
utilities for functionality beyond that provided by the shell itself -
such as \f[V]sed\f[R], and \f[V]awk\f[R].
In some systems the default versions of these utilities are not
sufficiently \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] compliant - i.e.\ they do not provide the
functionality required by the libraries.
For many such systems, more capable versions of these utilities
\f[I]are\f[R] available, only they are not the default tool found via
\f[V]PATH\f[R].
.PP
While it would be possible to provide configuration points for each such
utility used, this quickly becomes unwieldy and can somewhat obfuscate
the implementation details.
.PP
Instead, workarounds are provided for some of the known issues for
utilities - often by way of using a different utility.
These are automatically enabled when required, but can also be forced if
automatic detection fails.
This works well in many cases, but it requires a reasonable workaround
be available, which is not always true.
.PP
For any system that has a default utility that is not sufficiently
capable but that has an alternative version available that \f[I]is\f[R]
it is relatively trivial to ensure the libraries use the more capable
tool \f[I]without\f[R] requiring specific configuration options:
.IP \[bu] 2
Set \f[V]PATH\f[R]:
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
the most obvious method to accomplish this is to alter the
\f[V]PATH\f[R] variable to ensure the more capable tool is located
before the alternative
.IP \[bu] 2
this can be changed at system level, or just at script level
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
Use an alias:
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
For example: \f[V]alias awk=/usr/local/bin/nawk\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
defining an alias that has the same name as the utility will cause the
alias to be invoked \f[I]instead\f[R] of the utility
.IP \[bu] 2
note than some shells disable alias expansion in non-interactive shells
by default (e.g.\ \f[V]bash\f[R])
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
Use a function:
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
For example:
\f[V]awk() { echo \[aq]Using nawk\[aq] >&2; /usr/local/bin/nawk \[dq]$\[at]\[dq]; }\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
mostly equivalent to an alias, but aliases are limited in functionality
.RE
.PP
In all cases, provided these configurations are in place when the given
utility is \f[B]invoked\f[R] they alternative will be used instead.[13]
.SS PATTERN MATCHING
.PP
TL;DR:
.IP \[bu] 2
Pattern matching using wildcards, or Regular Expressions is supported
for some commands in libraries;
.IP \[bu] 2
Support for specific expressions is implementation dependent;
.IP \[bu] 2
In particular, Wildcard patterns will use a fallback implementation in
certain circumstances - this is significantly slower, but required to
provide portable behavior;
.IP \[bu] 2
The locale in effect changes what an expression will match - it is
\f[B]not\f[R] possible to alter the locale for a currently running
shell.
.PP
Details:
.PP
Some commands support pattern matching in various situations.
Such commands support either wildcards, or regular expressions, or more
often both.
.PP
The type of pattern matching used is specified the same way in all
libraries, via either a \f[V]grep\f[R] like option:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-E\f[R], \f[V]--ere\f[R], or \f[V]--extended-regexp\f[R], for
\[lq]Extended Regular
Expressions\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/basedefs/V1_chap09.html#tag_09_04)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-G\f[R], \f[V]--bre\f[R], or \f[V]--basic-regexp\f[R], for
\[lq]Basic Regular
Expressions\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/basedefs/V1_chap09.html#tag_09_03)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-W\f[R], \f[V]--glob\f[R], or \f[V]--wildcard\f[R], for
\[lq]Pattern Matching
Notation\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/utilities/V3_chap02.html#tag_18_13)
.PP
or a \f[V]test\f[R] like primary:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-[not]ere\f[R], \f[V]-[not]matchex\f[R], \f[V]-[not]matchere\f[R],
\f[V]=\[ti]\f[R], or \f[V]!\[ti]\f[R] for \[lq]Extended Regular
Expressions\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/basedefs/V1_chap09.html#tag_09_04)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-[not]bre\f[R], \f[V]-[not]match\f[R] and \f[V]-[not]matchbre\f[R],
for \[lq]Basic Regular
Expressions\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/basedefs/V1_chap09.html#tag_09_03)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-[not]like\f[R] for \[lq]Pattern Matching
Notation\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/utilities/V3_chap02.html#tag_18_13)
.PP
In some cases the test like primaries also support flags that affect how
the comparison is performed, these flags are specified as a suffix to
the primary, following a single \f[V]:\f[R] (\f[V]<colon>\f[R])
character:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]s\f[R] (as in \f[V]-bre:s\f[R]) specifies \f[I]Single Line
Mode\f[R];
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]m\f[R] (as in \f[V]-ere:m\f[R]) specifies \f[I]Multi-line
Mode\f[R].
.PP
In \f[I]Single Line Mode\f[R] some operations can be made significantly
faster, however it is not possible to match \f[V]<newline>\f[R]
characters (or match across line breaks), while \f[I]Multi-line
Mode\f[R] has no such restrictions.
If no suffix is specified, \f[V]m\f[R] is implied.[14]
.PP
\f[I]Note that using \f[VI]s\f[I] \f[R]may\f[I] \f[BI]decrease\f[I]
performance if values contain\f[R] \f[I]\f[VI]<newline>\f[I] characters,
although this usage is supported.\f[R]
.PP
\f[I]PORTABILITY\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
From testing it seems that in many cases \[lq]Basic Regular
Expressions\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/basedefs/V1_chap09.html#tag_09_03)
tend to be the most consistently well supported across implementations,
in addition they also are often faster than \[lq]Extended Regular
Expressions\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/basedefs/V1_chap09.html#tag_09_04)
and the fallback implementations for wildcards.
As such, for maximum portability with the best performance in all cases
it is advisable to use \[lq]Basic Regular
Expressions\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/basedefs/V1_chap09.html#tag_09_03)
when possible, however, it remains important to test specific
expressions on any platform they may be used.
.SS PATTERN MATCHING: WILDCARDS
.PP
TL;DR:
.IP \[bu] 2
Wildcard patterns are implemented using the shell whenever possible -
the locale the shell was invoked with determines what can be matched;
.IP \[bu] 2
Support for specific expressions is implementation dependent;
.IP \[bu] 2
Fallback implementations are provided for shell implementations that do
not handle wildcard patterns as expected - this is significantly slower;
.IP \[bu] 2
Expressions either match an \f[I]entire value\f[R] or do not match
(i.e.\ exactly like \f[V]case\f[R]).
.PP
Details:
.PP
\f[I]Wildcard\f[R] pattern matching, also known as \f[I]globbing\f[R],
is defined in the standard as \[lq]Pattern Matching
Notation\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/utilities/V3_chap02.html#tag_18_13),
and is implemented in the shell itself.[15]
.PP
Where library commands provide \f[I]wildcard\f[R] support:
.IP \[bu] 2
patterns \f[I]only\f[R] match if they match an \f[I]entire\f[R] value
(i.e.\ the pattern is \[lq]anchored\[rq] to both the start and end of a
value);
.IP \[bu] 2
only the characters \f[V]*\f[R] (\f[V]<asterisk>\f[R]), \f[V]?\f[R]
(\f[V]<question mark>\f[R]), and \f[V][\f[R]
(\f[V]<left-square-bracket>\f[R]) are special - all other characters are
literal;[16]
.IP \[bu] 2
patterns can match \f[V]<newline>\f[R] characters, either explicitly or
with the use of \f[V]*\f[R] (\f[V]<asterisk>\f[R]) or \f[V]?\f[R]
(\f[V]<question mark>\f[R]).
.PP
As a relatively simple form of matching being provided by the shell
itself, \f[I]wildcard\f[R] matching is often the best performing of the
pattern matching options.
.PP
\f[I]PORTABILITY\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Some characters are problematic for some implementations:\f[R]
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
the characters \f[V]\[rs]\f[R] (\f[V]<backslash>\f[R]), \f[V](\f[R]
(\f[V]<left-parenthesis>\f[R]), and \f[V])\f[R]
(\f[V]<right-parenthesis>\f[R]) are all known to be handled differently
in different implementations, making portable usage difficult;
.IP \[bu] 2
if a pattern uses one of these characters \f[I]and\f[R] the current
implementation does not process it as expected, \f[I]wildcard\f[R]
matching will be handled using the fallback implementation (see below).
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]Using a \f[VI]]\f[I] (\f[VI]<right-square-bracket>\f[I])
\f[BI]inside\f[I] a bracket expression (i.e.\f[R]
\f[I]\f[VI][...]...]\f[I]) is difficult (or impossible) to do
portably.\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Escaping characters can be achieved using \f[V][*]\f[R], \f[V][?]\f[R],
or \f[V][[]\f[R].
(This avoids the noted issues with \f[V]\[rs]\f[R]
(\f[V]<backslash>\f[R]).)
.IP \[bu] 2
Some implementations provide additional pattern matching operators using
specific sequences (commonly \f[V]*(...)\f[R], \f[V]+(...)\f[R],
\f[V]?(...)\f[R], \f[V]\[at](...)\f[R], \f[V]!(...)\f[R], though others
are possible).
These can generally be disabled/enabled using implementation specific
options settings and are often disabled by default.
These extensions \f[I]may\f[R] or \f[I]may not\f[R] affect the commands
in this library[17], to avoid any potential issues it is advisable to
write any such sequences as \f[V][*](...)\f[R], etc.
.IP \[bu] 2
The locale in effect for patten matching is \f[B]always\f[R] the locale
in effect when the shell was first invoked.
\f[I]It is \f[BI]not\f[I] possible to alter the\f[R] \f[I]locale of a
currently running shell.\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]In various situations \f[BI]wildcard\f[B] matching does not work as
might be expected\f[R] \f[B]- in these cases fallback code is used that
is markedly slower, but more\f[R] \f[B]predictable.\f[R]
.PP
Fallback implementation(s) are written using \f[V]awk\f[R] and are
required:
.IP \[bu] 2
for \f[I]wildcards\f[R] containing \f[V]\[rs]\f[R]
(\f[V]<backslash>\f[R]), \f[V](\f[R] (\f[V]<left-parenthesis>\f[R]), or
\f[V])\f[R] (\f[V]<right-parenthesis>\f[R]) \f[B]if\f[R] the current
shell does not handle these as expected;
.IP \[bu] 2
if the current shell does not support multi-byte characters correctly
with \f[I]wildcards\f[R] and the current locale is \f[I]not\f[R] the
\f[I]POSIX\f[R] locale;
.IP \[bu] 2
the shell was invoked in the \f[I]POSIX\f[R] locale but the current
locale is different.
.PP
Tests are performed when a library is sourced to determine if any of
these apply.
(See BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_SHELL_SUPPORTS_PORTABLE_GLOB and
BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_SHELL_SUPPORTS_MBC.)
.SS PATTERN MATCHING: REGULAR EXPRESSIONS
.PP
TL;DR:
.IP \[bu] 2
Regular Expression patterns are implemented using \f[V]awk\f[R],
\f[V]sed\f[R], or \f[V]grep\f[R] depending on the use case and
configuration;
.IP \[bu] 2
Support for specific expressions is implementation dependent;
.IP \[bu] 2
Different utilities \f[I]can and do\f[R] support different expressions;
.IP \[bu] 2
In contrast to Wildcard patterns, the locale in use \f[I]can\f[R] be
modified.
.PP
Details:
.PP
Both \[lq]Basic Regular
Expressions\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/basedefs/V1_chap09.html#tag_09_03)
(\f[I]BRE\f[R]) and \[lq]Extended Regular
Expressions\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/basedefs/V1_chap09.html#tag_09_04)
(\f[I]ERE\f[R]) are supported.
.PP
Patterns are \f[B]not\f[R] anchored by default and will match if
\f[B]any\f[R] part of the value matches the regular expression
(e.g.\ the expression \f[V].\f[R] will always match any value containing
one or more characters).
.PP
In \f[I]Multi-line Mode\f[R], \f[I]BRE\f[R] are implemented using
\f[V]sed\f[R], while \f[I]ERE\f[R] use \f[V]awk\f[R].[18]
.PP
In \f[I]Single Line Mode\f[R], both \f[I]BRE\f[R] and \f[I]ERE\f[R] use
\f[V]grep\f[R].
.PP
Note that as different tools are utilized in each mode it is possible
that support for specific expressions may differ (e.g.\ on Solaris the
default \f[V]awk\f[R] does not support \f[I]ERE\f[R] that the default
\f[V]grep\f[R] supports).
.PP
Note that in multi-line mode anchors will match the start
(\f[V]\[ha]\f[R]) and end (\f[V]$\f[R]) of the \f[I]entire\f[R] value
(i.e.\ not the start and end of a line).[19]
.PP
\f[I]PORTABILITY\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Different modes are implemented using different tools, as such behavior
\f[I]may\f[R] be different between modes (e.g.\ supported expressions
may differ).
.IP \[bu] 2
Although literal \f[V]<newline>\f[R] characters are permitted in both
\f[I]BRE\f[R] and \f[I]ERE\f[R] (for supported commands), it is highly
advisable to use \f[V]\[rs]n\f[R] (i.e.
\f[V]<slash>n\f[R]) instead.
.IP \[bu] 2
Both \f[I]BRE\f[R] and \f[I]ERE\f[R] are subject to a great many
portability issues, with some implementations being significantly less
capable than the standard requires.
The \f[V]autoconf\f[R]
docs (https://www.gnu.org/savannah-checkouts/gnu/autoconf/manual/html_node/Portable-Shell.html)
contains possibly the most extensive list of issues: see
\f[V]awk\f[R] (https://www.gnu.org/savannah-checkouts/gnu/autoconf/manual/html_node/Limitations-of-Usual-Tools.html#awk),
\f[V]grep\f[R] (https://www.gnu.org/savannah-checkouts/gnu/autoconf/manual/html_node/Limitations-of-Usual-Tools.html#grep),
and
\f[V]sed\f[R] (https://www.gnu.org/savannah-checkouts/gnu/autoconf/manual/html_node/Limitations-of-Usual-Tools.html#sed).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]BRE\f[R] seem to be more consistently well supported than
\f[I]ERE\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
The complexity of an expression can change how portable it is - of
particular note are optional (or null) matches, which can trip up less
capable engines.
\f[I]These seldom produce errors\f[R], instead only incorrect matches
are produced.
.SS PERFORMANCE
.PP
TL;DR:
.IP \[bu] 2
All libraries try to balance performance, portability, and
configurability;
.IP \[bu] 2
Certain configuration options can alter performance;
.IP \[bu] 2
The largest single factor affecting performance is the implementation(s)
of the utilities used.
.PP
Details:
.IP \[bu] 2
Libraries have been written to maximize performance without sacrificing
configurability, safety or utility - with a general philosophy of
\[lq]you don\[cq]t pay for what you don\[cq]t use\[rq].
.IP \[bu] 2
For \f[I]most\f[R] use cases library performance should not be an issue
and will likely be far outweighed by other factors.
.IP \[bu] 2
Where library performance \f[I]is\f[R] an issue, configuration of each
library can have a significant affect on performance.
Where configuration is known to affect performance, this is noted.
.IP \[bu] 2
The most significant factor in the performance of any library is the
specific external commands used by a library:
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
The shell used is the single most significant factor, for example,
\f[V]bash\f[R] is highly user friendly and provides many advanced tools
beyond those required by the standard, however the much less well
specified \f[V]dash\f[R] performs significantly better for all suite
libraries.
.IP \[bu] 2
Utilities like \f[V]sed\f[R], \f[V]grep\f[R], \f[V]awk\f[R], etc.
are available in multiple implementations, each of which has it\[cq]s
own performance characteristics.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
Many libraries provide emulated versions of data structures that are not
normally available.
These are stored in in a standard shell variable which is manipulated
using standard utilities or the shell command language.
\f[B]Performance of these data structures is highly dependent on
the\f[R] \f[B]size of the data stored.\f[R] Although implementation
dependent, shells tend to be optimized for processing \f[I]short\f[R]
strings, with strings that may be hundreds or thousands of characters
long performance can rapidly decrease.
.SS IMPLEMENTATION
.PP
There are many choices made in the implementation of the tools that may
seem unusual, however, largely these choices have been made for good
reason.
.PP
Of particular note are the extensive use of \f[V]case\f[R] where
\f[V]test\f[R] might be expected and the use of quotes where they may
not be required.
.PP
The extensive use of \f[V]case\f[R] in place of \f[V]test\f[R] is for
reasons of performance: replacing \f[V]test\f[R] with \f[V]case\f[R] has
a measurable impact on the performance of the tools, sometimes
dramatically so.
.SS PRIOR ART (OR WHY YET ANOTHER SHELL LIBRARY?)
.PP
There are literally hundreds, perhaps thousands, of libraries for shell
programming.
A search for any of \f[V]libsh\f[R], \f[V]shlib\f[R],
\f[V]libshell\f[R], \f[V]shelllib\f[R], \f[V]shboost\f[R], or
\f[V]shellboost\f[R], for example will return dozens upon dozens of
libraries - most of which are designed for shell programming.
However, the quality, scope, and usefulness of these varies greatly,
with common issues including: targeting a limited number of shells;
limited scope; poor documentation; poor portability.[20]
.PP
While many of these libraries are undoubtedly useful, even the best
available were found to be, in one way or another, unsuitable for the
purposes that \f[V]shtoolkit\f[R] now exists to fulfill.
.SS AI
.PP
\f[B]\f[BI]No library code was originated by AI.\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
AI has been used in the following ways:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]Auditing:\f[R] all code and documentation (including this file) has
been audited by AI for correctness, including but not limited to
checking for bugs and security issues, verifying that comments
accurately reflect the code they document, and confirming that
documentation is written clearly and unambiguously.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]Fixes:\f[R] where an audit identifies an issue, AI will issue a
fix.
Every change is fully reviewed before being accepted (and not every fix
\f[I]is\f[R] accepted); nothing is committed unreviewed.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]Tests:\f[R] most tests are hand-written; some have been added by
AI, typically simple additions extending existing coverage where it was
previously missing.
.PP
All libraries are written first by hand, to a fully functional level -
only once a library is felt to be near release ready is AI involved (in
the ways noted above).
.PP
Note that while there is significant disagreement over the use of AI in
general, and it is, understandably, anathema for many, its use has
permitted \f[V]shtoolkit\f[R] to be maintained and updated to an extent
that would otherwise not have been possible (due to a number of
factors).
.SS CAVEATS
.PP
The tools and libraries in \f[V]shtoolkit\f[R] are subject to the
limitations imposed by the particular environment in which they are
invoked.
Each implementation of the required utilities and command execution
environment will have specific limitations that may be different to
those in another implementation and may change between versions of the
same utilities.[21]
.PP
For most use cases it is deemed unlikely that these limitations will be
an issue, however, there will be cases where some limitations may cause
problems.
.SS COMMAND LINE LENGTH LIMIT
.PP
It is impossible to determine all the possible limitations that may
exist or may be of issue (even when considering only those specified in
the standard).
However, of the known limitations, perhaps the most likely to be
encountered across multiple libraries is the command line length limit,
which can be encountered in a number of scenarios, and in unexpected
ways.
.PP
The standard specifies this as
\f[V]{ARG_MAX}\f[R] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/functions/execl.html)
and defines it as:
.RS
.PP
The number of bytes available for [a] new process\[cq] combined argument
and environment lists\&...
It is implementation-defined whether null terminators, pointers, and/or
any alignment bytes are included in this total.
.RE
.PP
The value for any particular environment can be queried using the
command \f[V]getconf ARG_MAX\f[R], though this value can only be used as
a guide since it is impossible to know how many bytes any command will
require in advance - indeed the command line length limit does
\f[I]not\f[R] apply to all commands: this limit does not apply to any
shell builtin commands.[22]
.PP
For a modern system the value of the command line length limit can be
several million bytes, while older systems it can be significantly less,
though it \f[I]is\f[R] possible to increase the available command line
length for commands, by, for example:
.IP \[bu] 2
reducing the number (and size) of exported variables;
.IP \[bu] 2
avoiding characters that use more than a single byte;
.IP \[bu] 2
changing system settings to increase the size of \f[V]ARG_MAX\f[R].[23]
.PP
Importantly, any variable which is exported and also has it\[cq]s
\f[I]contents\f[R] used as an argument to a command will count
\f[B]TWICE\f[R] towards this limit.
.PP
It is recommended that variables containing library data are
\f[I]not\f[R] exported.
.SS LINUX \f[V]MAX_ARG_STRLEN\f[R]
.PP
While the Linux kernel uses \f[V]ARG_MAX\f[R] as defined in the
standard, it adds an additional restriction which is \f[B]not\f[R]
standard - each argument passed to a command is subject to a maximum
length as defined by \f[V]MAX_ARG_STRLEN\f[R].
Sadly, this value can not be directly queried in the shell, but appears
to be hardcoded in the kernel as \f[V]PAGE_SIZE * 32\f[R] which on many
(all?)
systems appears to equate to \f[V]131072\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]Any single argument larger than this can never be passed to a
command in Linux.\f[R]
.SS INTERNAL COMMANDS & VARIABLES
.PP
All libraries require a number of internal commands and variables to
provide the provided functionality, these are distinguishable from other
values by a prefix: commands these are prefixed with \f[V]fn_bs_\f[R];
while variables are prefixed with \f[V]g_BS_\f[R], \f[V]c_BS_\f[R], or
\f[V]i_BS\f[R].
.PP
These are \f[B]strictly\f[R] for internal usage and \f[B]must not\f[R]
be invoked or referenced outside the library to which they belong.
.SS SEE ALSO
.PP
getarg(1), libgetargs(7), libarray(7), libdeque(7), libpath(7)
.SH NOTES
.SS [1]
.PP
While it would be possible to set some environment variables to the
required values when needed by a specific library (e.g.\ setting the
\f[V]POSIX\f[R] locale), this is not always easy to do while avoiding
changing the state for the invoker \f[I]and\f[R] maintaining
performance.
Setting all variables as part of a command might be possible in many
cases, but would require huge lines of code for each command, and
setting variables may not even be possible (e.g.\ standard variables may
be \f[V]readonly\f[R] in a restricted shell, while utilities like
\f[V]env\f[R] can not be used for shell builtins).
Finally, setting these variables to the expected value assumes that
other values do not work, which may not be true and may make some uses
of the libraries impossible without any real need.
.SS [2]
.PP
Some shells and utilities (most notably those provided by \f[I]GNU\f[R])
add additional locale variables to those specified by the standard.
These are mostly similar to \f[V]LC_MESSAGES\f[R], and do not affect
general behavior of utilities, however the variable \f[V]LANGUAGE\f[R]
\f[I]may\f[R] cause issues in some circumstances.
.SS [3]
.PP
There are numerous subtle differences between the UTF-8 versions and the
other versions, for example, character classes will contain many more
characters in the UTF-8 version.
Standard conforming tools need not provide standard conforming behavior
for these locales.
.SS [4]
.PP
It is not possible to have literal multi-byte characters in a script
file as not all shells can process these (leading to errors on
sourcing).
The only real way to get a multi- byte character is to use \f[V]awk\f[R]
which supports octal escapes, however, code points are not unique in
locales, leading to problems as it is not possible to determine what
character a specific octal escape sequence represents.
For a significant time now, UTF-8 seems to be the defacto standard for
character encodings, with UTF-8 locales available for most modern
systems.
Given all this, any tests for multi-byte support assume UTF-8 code
points.
In the case where the locale is \f[I]NOT\f[R] UTF-8 this will likely
fail and manual configuration will be required.
Note that failure to chose the correct tool will often \f[I]only\f[R]
result in less optimal performance and is, in most cases, unlikely to
result in errors - the main exception to this is for systems that have
inconsistent support for multi-byte characters (particularly for
\f[V]awk\f[R]).
.SS [5]
.PP
Delayed detection is helpful in some cases, particularly when locale is
important - without delayed detection the best code path may not be
chosen (as the locale may not be set appropriately).
Note that delayed detection only ever allows a better code path to be
chosen - it should \f[B]not\f[R] cause errors if skipped.
.SS [6]
.PP
In some cases it is not possible to detect issues with 100% accuracy,
while some tests may cause measurable delays for short scripts - in
these cases setting the appropriate variable correctly is recommended.
.SS [7]
.PP
Technically since the default configuration of \f[I]Z Shell\f[R] is
non-standard it is not supported, however this work-around is provided
since it can be easily scoped, and does not notably affect performance,
and causes no issues with other environments.
Similar work-arounds for other environments are not always possible
(e.g.\ the GNU specific
\f[V]POSIXLY_CORRECT\f[R] (https://www.gnu.org/prep/standards/html_node/Non_002dGNU-Standards.html)
environment variable can not so easily be dealt with).
.SS [8]
.PP
The \f[V]local\f[R] keyword is widely supported but as it is non-
standard how it behaves varies between implementations.
Many implementations of \f[V]local\f[R] \f[I]may\f[R] work for these
cases, however some implementations will not.
.SS [9]
.PP
Legacy systems and software can often be found in older organizations,
especially where the organizations (and hence the associated systems)
are somewhat specialized.
While many of these systems are no longer actively maintained by the
original manufactures, they continue to be used.
Even those systems that are still maintained may contain long obsolete
software.
For example, \f[I]Oracle Solaris 11.4\f[R] shipped in September 2023,
yet contains a version of \f[V]ksh88\f[R] (i.e.\ the 1988 version of
KornShell) - although this shell is largely similar to more modern
shells it does deviate somewhat, here it simply serves as an example of
how even maintained systems can continue to support very old software.
\f[B]Where\f[R] \f[B]practicable\f[R], \f[I]the Suite\f[R] is intended
to support all such systems.
(\f[V]pdksh\f[R] is also worth mentioning - it continues to be supported
on some systems despite not having being meaningfully updated since last
century.)
.SS [10]
.PP
Legacy \[lq]Command
Substitution\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/utilities/V3_chap02.html#tag_18_06_03)
(i.e.
\f[V]\[ga]command\[ga]\f[R]) and arithmetic via \f[V]expr\f[R] may be
possible to support if there is significant interest in this, however,
\[lq]Parameter
Expansion\[rq] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/utilities/V3_chap02.html#tag_18_06_02)
is likely to always be required as there are no real alternatives that
do not also have significant drawbacks.
.SS [11]
.PP
While \f[V]sh\f[R] is often simply a link to another shell, this is not
always true - for some platforms it is a unique shell.
.SS [12]
.PP
Working around this issue is trivial: simply edit the scripts to target
a different shell; ensure \f[V]/usr/bin/env sh\f[R] invokes a suitably
capable shell; or only use the library versions of commands.
In the future install time detection and correction of these issues may
be added.
.SS [13]
.PP
These configurations options do \f[B]not\f[R] have any affect when a
library is \f[I]parsed\f[R], only when the code is actually
\f[I]invoked\f[R].
However, libraries \f[I]may\f[R] invoke utilities during sourcing in
order to determine which workarounds to use.
It is advisable to ensure these are configured \f[I]prior\f[R] to
sourcing to avoid any unexpected issues.
.SS [14]
.PP
The difference is effectively the difference between \f[V]grep\f[R] and
\f[V]awk\f[R] or \f[V]sed\f[R] - the latter can match patterns across
multiple lines, while the former can not.
.SS [15]
.PP
It is unfortunate that the standard uses the term \f[I]Pattern
Matching\f[R] to refer specifically to this form of pattern matching.
Except where referring directly to the standard, variations on the terms
\f[I]wildcard\f[R] or \f[I]glob\f[R] are preferred across the suite,
with the term \f[I]pattern matching\f[R] used as an umbrella term that
also includes regular expressions.
(Historically, \[lq]glob\[rq] was used only for file matching, however,
it is more widely applied now.)
.SS [16]
.PP
Note that \f[V]]\f[R] (\f[V]<right-square-bracket>\f[R]) is
\f[I]only\f[R] special if preceded by a \f[V][\f[R]
(\f[V]<left-square-bracket>\f[R]), while \f[V]|\f[R]
(\f[V]<vertical-line>\f[R]) is only special when used with
\f[V]case\f[R] (and is \f[I]not\f[R] special for library commands).
.SS [17]
.PP
Although pattern matching is used in several different places in the
shell (e.g.\ \f[V]case\f[R] matches, file expansion, etc.)
- each has very slightly different rules (e.g.
\f[V]case\f[R] supports \f[V]|\f[R] while file expansion does not).
Whether or not any extensions will affect specific pattern matching is
implementation defined, and not always clearly indicated.
.SS [18]
.PP
Although \f[V]expr\f[R] is faster than \f[V]sed\f[R] for \f[I]BRE\f[R]
matching, it is hard to use portably and has many limitations that do
not affect \f[V]sed\f[R] - as such it is not used for pattern matching
as it can not be guaranteed to function as expected.
.SS [19]
.PP
Although this seems widely supported for the utilities described by the
standard, more generally this is an area where regular expression
implementations often diverge, which often leads to subtle and difficult
to diagnose bugs.
Use with care.
.SS [20]
.PP
It would be impossible to list even a fraction of those that are
available, however the \[lq]Awesome Shell\[rq]
list (https://github.com/alebcay/awesome-shell) provides a curated list
of some of the better libraries.
.SS [21]
.PP
Many of these limitations are specified in the standard, with specific
constraints, though generally the actual value is \[lq]implementation
defined\[rq].
.SS [22]
.PP
Given that each shell implementation has a different set of commands
that are builtin this means that when and where this limit may be hit is
very difficult to determine.
Additionally some commands are available as both builtin and standalone
commands - it is possible to invoke either but only the standalone
command is subject to the command line length limitation.
For a shell like \f[V]busybox\f[R] \f[I]everything\f[R] can be a builtin
command and the command line length limits may not apply.
.SS [23]
.PP
On at least some systems, the size of \f[V]ARG_MAX\f[R] is tied to other
system settings and can be increased by modifying those configurables.
This must be done with care, however, as these values affect how many
different systems interact and may cause issues that are not immediately
obvious.
